    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d106-a1">

                <pb facs="0284" n="156" sameAs="frus:frus1885/pg156"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 1 in No. 650.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Wingate to Mr.
                            Young</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1885-01-26"><hi rendition="#small-caps">January</hi> 26,
                            1885</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 115.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I do not usually enter communications like
                    the No. 327 received in my letter-book. Such letters and business circulars are
                    usually treated as unofficial. If I can give any real information to the sender
                    I answer them, but most received are evidently so ill adapted to the demand of
                    this people that I think I am right in concluding that they are merely sent to
                    the consulate because the sender finds the name of the place in the consular
                    directory, and not as expecting an acknowledgment.</p>
                <p>In a case like the one under consideration I should, if a proper opportunity
                    offered, speak to an official regarding a manufacture by citizens of the United
                    States, but I could not with propriety go further.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">* * * * * * *</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>J. C. A. WINGATE.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
